        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794458028.7626405
      },
      "rev_n_6_d4c444_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_d4c444_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_d4c444",
        "thought_text": "Use modular arithmetic to simplify the problem and determine the state of the wave at time t.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1734514200719101246",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794458028.762656
      },
      "rev_n_6_d4c444_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_d4c444_1",
        "node_id": "n_6_d4c444",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2 (revised): To accurately determine the number of standing spectators at time \\( t \\), we need to consider both the position of the wave and its direction. Specifically, we should calculate the effective position of the wave within the cycle and then determine if it is moving upwards or downwards. If the wave is moving upwards, the number of standing spectators will increase until it reaches the midpoint of the cycle, and then decrease. Conversely, if the wave is moving downwards, the number of standing spectators will decrease until it reaches the midpoint, and then increase. We can use modular arithmetic to find the effective position and then apply this logic to compute the number of standing spectators.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1734514200719101246",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_6_d4c444_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_8d740b8e29",
